@page "/"
@using Lms.Application.Services
@inject ICourseService CourseService

<PageTitle>Home</PageTitle>

<h1>Real Estate Certification LMS</h1>
<p>Welcome to the starter LMS experience.</p>

@if (_courses is null)
{
    <p>Loading courses...</p>
}
else
{
    <div class="course-grid">
        @foreach (var course in _courses)
        {
            <div class="card">
                <h3>@course.Title</h3>
                <p>@course.Description</p>
                <a href="/courses/@course.Id">View details</a>
            </div>
        }
    </div>
}

<section class="licensing-faq" aria-labelledby="licensing-faq-heading">
    <div class="licensing-faq-header">
        <p class="course-kicker">North Carolina licensing guide</p>
        <h2 id="licensing-faq-heading">Common questions about broker licensing</h2>
        <p>Use these answers as a quick guide to the North Carolina broker licensing path. Always confirm current requirements with the North Carolina Real Estate Commission <a href="https://www.ncrec.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">https://www.ncrec.gov/</a>.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="licensing-faq-list">
        <details>
            <summary>How do I become a Provisional Broker (PB)?</summary>
            <p>Complete the 75-hour Broker Prelicensing Course, apply for a North Carolina broker license, satisfy the Commission's qualification and character requirements, and pass the license examination. A newly issued broker license is initially placed on provisional status, so the licensee is called a Provisional Broker.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
            <summary>Can a Provisional Broker work independently?</summary>
            <p>No. A PB must have an active license and be supervised by a designated Broker-in-Charge. The PB may not operate independently and may engage in licensed activity only through the required brokerage affiliation and supervision.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
            <summary>How does a PB become a Broker (B)?</summary>
            <p>Complete the 90-hour Postlicensing Education Program, generally within 18 months after initial licensure. Once the postlicensing requirement is satisfied, the provisional status is removed and the licensee becomes a non-provisional Broker without taking another license examination.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
            <summary>Does the 90-hour postlicensing program have a deadline?</summary>
            <p>Yes. The 90 hours must be completed within 18 months following initial licensure. Missing the requirement can place the license on inactive status until the requirements for returning to active status are met.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
            <summary>What is BIC Eligible status?</summary>
            <p>BIC Eligible is not a separate license. It is a status that allows a qualified, non-provisional Broker to apply to be designated as a Broker-in-Charge. The broker must have an active license, the required recent brokerage experience, and the 12-hour Broker-in-Charge Course.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
            <summary>What experience is required for BIC Eligible status?</summary>
            <p>The broker must have at least two years of full-time brokerage experience, or equivalent part-time experience, within the previous five years. There is also an equivalent North Carolina attorney pathway involving primarily real-estate-closing practice.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
            <summary>When should I take the 12-hour BIC Course?</summary>
            <p>The broker must complete the 12-hour Broker-in-Charge Course no earlier than one year before applying for BIC Eligible status and no later than 120 days after applying.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
            <summary>How do I become a designated Broker-in-Charge?</summary>
            <p>After obtaining BIC Eligible status, submit the BIC designation form for the sole proprietorship, firm, or branch office. Each firm generally designates one BIC for its principal office and one for each branch office. The designation is an office responsibility, not a new license type.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
            <summary>What does a designated BIC supervise?</summary>
            <p>The BIC is responsible for office supervision and administration, including supervising provisional brokers, overseeing advertising, maintaining required transaction and trust-account records, and helping affiliated brokers comply with agency and disclosure requirements.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
            <summary>What must a BIC do after assuming trust-account responsibility?</summary>
            <p>A designated BIC responsible for a trust account must complete the Commission's Basic Trust Account Procedures Course within 120 days, unless the BIC completed it within the prior three years.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
            <summary>Is “broker,” “agent,” or “REALTOR” the license type?</summary>
            <p>North Carolina has one real estate license type: the broker license. PB, Broker, and BIC are license status categories or designations. “Agent” is commonly used for a licensee acting for a party, while REALTOR is a private trade-association membership and is not automatically granted with a license.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
            <summary>How should I keep my license current?</summary>
            <p>Complete postlicensing first to remove provisional status and become a Broker. After that, track continuing education, license renewal, and any BIC-related course or office deadlines. License status can change when education or renewal requirements are missed, so keep completion records and verify current dates and requirements directly with NCREC.</p>
        </details>
    </div>

</section>

<section class="licensing-faq" aria-labelledby="course-faq-heading">
    <div class="licensing-faq-header">
        <p class="course-kicker">Course and account guide</p>
        <h2 id="course-faq-heading">Common questions about taking a course</h2>
        <p>Find quick answers about enrollment, course access, progress, assessments, certificates, and account support.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="licensing-faq-list">
        <details>
            <summary>How do I enroll in a course?</summary>
            <p>Browse the course catalog, open a course to review its details, and follow the enrollment or purchase steps shown on the course page. Once enrollment is complete, the course appears in your learner workspace.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
            <summary>How do I start my course?</summary>
            <p>Sign in, open your learner workspace, select an enrolled course, and choose Start Course. Required lessons, checkpoints, assessments, and any configured course time limits will be shown as you move through the course.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
            <summary>Can I continue where I left off?</summary>
            <p>Yes. Lesson completion and course progress are saved to your enrollment. Return to the course from your learner workspace and continue from the highest lesson position you have unlocked.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
            <summary>What does course progress measure?</summary>
            <p>Progress reflects completed required lessons and course requirements. Some courses also require passing checkpoints or a final assessment before completion can be recorded.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
            <summary>What is a course deadline?</summary>
            <p>A course deadline is the date by which the course should be completed. You may receive reminders before the deadline and an overdue notification after it passes. Check your learner workspace for the date assigned to your enrollment.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
            <summary>What happens if I miss a course deadline?</summary>
            <p>The enrollment is marked overdue and remains visible for follow-up. Contact the school or administrator to ask whether the deadline can be adjusted or the enrollment can be corrected.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
            <summary>Do I have to complete every lesson?</summary>
            <p>Complete all required lessons and any required checkpoints or assessments configured for the course. A certificate is issued only when the course completion requirements have been satisfied.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
            <summary>Do courses require an assessment or proctored exam?</summary>
            <p>Requirements depend on the course. A course may include a final assessment, checkpoint questions, or a proctored examination. The course details and lesson flow identify the requirements that apply.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
            <summary>What happens if I fail an assessment?</summary>
            <p>The course records the attempt and shows the available result. Depending on the course policy, you may have another attempt after a cooldown period or after an administrator grants additional attempts.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
            <summary>How do I receive my completion certificate?</summary>
            <p>When all required course and assessment conditions are met, the system issues a completion certificate. You can review eligible certificates from the certificate area and use the public verification process when a third party needs to confirm one.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
            <summary>How do I reset my password?</summary>
            <p>Use the account support process or contact an administrator if you cannot sign in. An administrator can reset the password, after which the account may require a password change at the next sign-in.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
            <summary>Who should I contact when I need help?</summary>
            <p>Contact the school or administrator for enrollment, deadline, course, certificate, or licensing-record questions. Include your name, account email, course title, and a short description of the issue so it can be resolved efficiently.</p>
        </details>
    </div>
</section>

@code {
    private List<Lms.Domain.Entities.Course> _courses = new();

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        _courses = await CourseService.GetPublishedCoursesAsync();
    }
}
